\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Croatia cenbank buys euros to ease firming pressures on kuna  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2020\par 14:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 ZAGREB, Dec 15 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Croatian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it had bought 130 million euros ($157.78 million) from commercial banks in an auction to relieve upward pressure on the kuna \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It said it had bought the euros at an average rate of 7.5360 kuna. The kuna was quoted at 7.5330 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from around 7.5130 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 In March, as the COVID-19 pandemic hit \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to counter depreciation pressures on the kuna and sold more than two billion euros to the banks.\par \par \uc2 The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying the euros from the commercial banks was in August 2019.\par \par \uc2 This year Croatia entered the European Exchange Mechanism (ERM-2), a waiting room of at least two years before adopting the euro. Zagreb hopes to meet all the macroeconomic criteria, like the budget gap or the public debt/GDP ratio, on time to join the euro zone in 2023.\par \par \uc2 As part of ERM-2, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) set the central rate of the kuna at 7.5345 per one euro ($1 = 0.8239 euros) (Reporting by Igor Ilic Editing by William Maclean) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-12-15T14:36:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201215egcf028mt