\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Croatian assets surge to multi-year highs as economy improves\b0\par\par\uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2017\par 11:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Recovering economy boosts kuna, dealers watch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Zloty touches new 2-month high, stocks firm\par     * Price moves muted as investors await U.S. payroll data\par     * Romanian assets off lows but edgy due to political turmoil\par \par     By Sandor Peto and Igor Ilic\par     BUDAPEST/ZAGREB, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Croatia's kuna, buoyed by\par an improving economy, set its firmest level in over 4 years\par against the euro on Friday, while central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par were mixed.\par     The region's assets got support in recent days from the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as neither of the\par banks signaled tighter than expected policies.\par     The kuna, which like the zloty gradually gained\par ground in the past weeks, was the strongest since October 2012\par at 7.436 on Friday. Dealers said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in the market any time to stem the rally.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Croatia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which enjoys a robust tourism sector, has current\par account surpluses. Inflation, near zero, has not been a worry so\par far and the economy is also on an upswing.\par     The Croatian economy returned to growth last year after a\par weak decade. Retail sales data published on Thursday showed a \par robust 5.9 percent annual rise. \par     The equities indices of Warsaw and the much smaller\par Zagreb bourse have been the region's top gainers this\par year, with 6.4 percent and 8.5 percent rise, respectively. On\par Friday the Zagreb index was the highest since August 2011.\par     The Croatian government also opened books on Friday for\par domestic bond issues, one of the lead arrangers said, to\par refinance a 5.5-billion-kuna ($800-million) 10-year bond,\par maturing on Feb. 8. \par     Elsewhere in the region, investors were on the sidelines,\par awaiting more economic data from the United States.\par     "Investors are cautious now ahead of the U.S. non-farm\par payroll figures," one Budapest-based fixed income trader said.\par Those figures are due at 1330 GMT.\par     Hungarian and Polish bond yields rose 1-2 basis points.\par     The forint was a tad weaker against the euro at\par 0936 GMT after data showing disappointing 3.2 percent annual\par rise in retail sales in December. The zloty set a new\par 2-month high at 4.299.\par     Romanian assets were off lows hit after a decree passed on\par Tuesday triggered massive street protests against corruption\par this week, which are likely to continue, and cracks in the\par one-month-old government.  \par     While inflation in Croatia is still hovering near zero, it\par has rebounded in other countries of the region.\par     Czech inflation has reached the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target in\par December. If the January data due on Feb. 10 are high, that may\par refuel speculation that the bank could soon abandon its cap at\par 27 against the euro soon.\par     The crown's exchange rate implied in six-month forward deals\par  indicated that recent comments from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - including after Thursday's meeting - have tamed\par speculation for early exit from the cap and a crown surge.\par  \par     The implied rate reached a 4-week low on Friday at 26.869\par 