\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  and bonds ease ahead of talks on Greek debt\b0\par\par\uc2 883 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2015\par 16:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * CEE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , bonds ease as euro group discusses Greek\par bailout\par     * Crown eases, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief defends weak crown policy\par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Croatian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells euro after kuna falls to\par 11-year low\par     * Serbian buys euro to stem dinar surge ahead of bond\par auctions\par \par  (Recasts with fall in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )\par     By Sandor Peto and Jakub Iglewski\par     BUDAPEST/WARSAW, Feb 20  (Reuters) - Central European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government bonds fell on Friday as concern grew\par over Greece's future in the euro zone, the main export market of\par the region's emerging economies.\par     Later today, euro zone finance ministers will meet on\par whether to extend a loan agreement that Greece urgently needs.\par Its current bailout programme expires on Feb. 28. \par     "Greece is a No. 1 topic right now," one Warsaw-based\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer said. "All that investors care about is to close\par riskier positions before the weekend. Risk-off attitude is\par dominating emerging markets today and the zloty is losing, too."\par     The zloty slipped 0.4 percent against the euro by\par 1432 GMT. The forint, which earlier in the day traded\par near a three-month high, shed 0.3 percent to 306.35. The leu\par  eased 0.2 percent to 4.458. \par     Government bond yields rose, with Polish 10-year debt\par trading at 2.33 percent, up 7 basis points and Hungary's\par corresponding yield rising 1 basis point to 3.27 percent,\par reversing an early drop.\par     Analysts have said that the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par relatively high-yielding government bonds could gain if Greece\par reaches a deal on its debt.\par     The Czech crown may be an exception. It decoupled\par from its regional peers this week and got on a roller coaster\par after Czech President Milos Zeman slammed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par "devaluation" policy. \par     The bank's governor, Miroslav Singer, reacted late on\par Thursday, saying that the bank will not end its policy of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if necessary to keep the crown weak before the\par second half of 2016. \par     The crown eased half a percent against the euro to 27.531. \par     "In the next couple of days I see the crown in the range\par 27.400-700," one Prague-based dealer said. "The main factor is\par Greece, of course. The market is quite nervous because of it." \par     The kuna bucked the regional trend, strengthening\par by 0.2 percent against the euro to 7.713, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Croatian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold 172.7 million euros to commercial banks. It\par was the bank's second \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year to support the\par kuna. \par     Companies in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Croatia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been buying euros, while its\par economy has contracted by about 13 percent in the past six\par years. \par     Neighbouring Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in contrast, sold euros\par to stem a surge by the dinar, which it keeps in tight ranges.\par The dinar still gained 0.7 percent to 120.14 per euro.\par     The Serbian economy has also underperformed Central European\par peers, but demand from foreign investors lifted the dinar before\par next week's auctions of high-yielding 1-year and 3-year bonds,\par dealers said. \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1532 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2015 \par  Czech crown                 27.531    27.388   -0.52%  +0.44% \par  Hungary forint             306.350   305.500   -0.28%  +3.30% \par  Polish zloty                 4.184     4.168   -0.39%  +2.43% \par  Romanian leu                 4.458     4.447   -0.23%  +0.52% \par  Croatian kuna                7.713     7.726   +0.16%  -0.75% \par  Serbian dinar              120.140   121.010   +0.72%  +1.01% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par                                      close     change  in 2015 \par  Prague                      1014.73  1007.19   +0.75%  +6.64% \par  Budapest                   18023.85 18080.36   -0.31% +09.11% \par  Warsaw                      2349.63  2348.06   +0.07%  +1.14% \par  Bucharest                   7294.03  7285.89   +0.11%  +5.71% \par  Ljubljana                    781.26   780.56   +0.09%  +0.22% \par  Zagreb                      1751.11  1742.56   +0.49%  +0.63% \par  Belgrade                     661.17   662.70   -0.23%  +0.80% \par  Sofia                        487.67   483.29   +0.91%  -6.90% \par  **************