\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1-Assets in holding pattern as markets monitor Greek debt talks\b0\par\par\uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2015\par 11:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Progress at Greek debt talks could lift CEE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par bonds\par     * Crown eases, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief defends weak crown policy\par     * Kuna hits 11-year low, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls euro-selling\par auction\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Feb 20  (Reuters) - Central European assets were\par stuck in a holding pattern on Friday as investors awaited a\par meeting of euro zone finance ministers to discuss Greece's\par urgent need for more financial aid.\par     If a deal can be reached, the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par relatively high-yielding government bonds could climb. \par     Yields on debt in the region have risen in recent weeks from\par near record lows on concerns over debt-laden Greece's future in\par the euro zone.\par     Hungary sold almost 50 percent more government bonds than\par planned at its auctions on Thursday, however, with yields\par falling in the secondary market. The yield on three-year paper\par dropped a further six basis points on Friday to 2.18 percent.\par     "As already witnessed yesterday, the upward pressure, mainly\par on longer-dated LCY (local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) yields in the region, eased\par with hopes of a viable solution to the Greek debt crisis,"\par Raiffeisen said in a note.\par     Poland's zloty eased 0.2 percent against the euro\par by 0931 GMT while Hungary's forint firmed 0.1 percent\par to 305.06.\par     One trader said recent data showing good economic growth had\par buoyed the forint. Figures on Friday showed a 6.7 percent annual\par rise in gross wages in December. \par     The Czech crown has been on a rollercoaster in the\par last few days, decoupling from regional peers after Czech\par President Milos Zeman slammed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's "devaluation"\par policy. \par     The bank's governor, Miroslav Singer, reacted late on\par Thursday, saying that the bank will not end its policy of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if necessary to keep the crown weak before the\par second half of 2016. \par     The crown eased 0.2 percent against the euro to\par 27.435. \par     Raiffeisen said in its note that the crown could head back\par towards 27.7, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Komercni Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2  fixed income trader Dalimil\par Vyskovsky said it could settle around 27.35.\par     "We do not think another big drop would be a threat today,\par however, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market will be a crucial guide for trading rates\par in the next days, or weeks," he added.\par     Croatia's kuna touched 11-year lows against the\par euro at 7.733 in early trade. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  called an auction\par to sell euros in its second \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year to prop up\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Companies in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Croatia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been buying euros, while the\par economy has contracted by about 13 percent in the past six\par years. \par     \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1031 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \high