\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Polish bonds retreat; attention shifts to output data\b0\par\par\uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2014\par 17:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Polish data leave rate cut outlook unchanged\par     * CEE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , equities rebound, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  still eyed\par     * Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend dinar\par     * Floods hit Serbia, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Croatia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Sept 16 (Reuters) - Polish government bonds\par retreated on Tuesday after employment and corporate wage figures\par for August suggested the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not cut\par rates any more than expected.\par     The move also reflected a small rise in the yields of\par long-dated euro zone government debt.\par     Poland's 10-year benchmark yield rose to 3.07\par percent from a morning low at 3.03 percent. Earlier, Poland\par reported that wages rose 3.5 percent in August on an annual\par basis and employment continued to grow, albeit at a slightly\par slower pace of 0.7 percent.\par     Analysts said industrial output figures due on Wednesday may\par provide more clues about when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut\par its main interest rate, now 2.5 percent, and by how much. The\par consensus forecast is for 0.7 percent growth in output.\par     If the output figure disappoints, "it may fuel expectations\par that the overall (rate) cut will be bigger than we currently\par expect (i.e. 25 basis pints in October and another 25 basis\par points in November)," Erste said in a note.\par     Economic slowdown, partly due to the effects of conflict in\par Ukraine, have made the region's central banks, especially\par Poland's, increasingly dovish in the past months.\par     Concern that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may signal at its meeting\par on Wednesday that it will soon raise rates has weighed on\par long-dated bonds in recent weeks. But expectations the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will begin a programme of asset purchases have\par started to prop up bond prices, and yields are lower than they\par were six weeks ago.   \par     The average yield on the 3-month \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bills Hungary sold\par at an auction stayed at record lows at 1.38 percent. Longer debt\par yields even dropped by a few basis points in the secondary\par market in low turnover.\par     \par     CONCERNS EASE\par     Concern over Wednesday's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting and turmoil in Ukraine\par had eased by afternoon trade, and the region's equities and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mostly recovered from early declines.\par     The loss of the stocks of Hungarian pharmaceuticals Richter\par , which exports to Russia and Ukraine, narrowed to 0.36\par percent from more than 1 percent. Budapest's main stock index\par  gained 0.25 percent, although turnover remained low.\par     Prague's main stock index was still down by 1.1\par percent, dragged lower mainly by a 2 percent decline of\par electricity producer CEZ PR>. CEZ fell on profit-taking;\par its shares had risen more than 10 percent since early August.\par     The shares of Slovenia's telecom operator, Telekom\par , jumped by more than 6 percent to 137.5 euros after\par incoming finance minister Dusan Mramor said late on Monday the\par privatisation of Telekom will continue. Ljubljana's blue-chip\par SBI index gained 1.9 pct. \par     The privatisation was put on hold earlier in September, \par awaiting approval of the sale by the new centre-left government\par that is expected to be sworn in on Thursday. \par     The dinar rose a third of a percent against the\par euro to 118.72, strongly outperforming regional peers, after\par hitting two-year lows a week ago because of concern over a delay\par in much-needed fiscal reforms in Serbia.\par     Dealers said the Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which repeatedly\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year to protect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , bought the dinar\par in the market again in early trade. \par     Croatia's kuna eased 0.1 percent.\par     One person died in Serbia and hundreds were evacuated due to\par new floods in the Balkans. Severe floods in May killed dozens\par and worsened Croatia's and Serbia's recession and fiscal\par problems.   \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1556 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.547    27.558   +0.04%  -0.78% \par  Hungary forint             314.250   314.120   -0.04%  -5.50% \par  Polish zloty                 4.197     4.198   +0.04%  -1.12% \par  Romanian leu                 4.415     4.410   -0.11%  +1.02% \par  Croatian kuna                7.625     7.620   -0.07%  -0.09% \par  Serbian dinar              118.720   119.120   +0.34%  -3.55% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par           