\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  just down, stocks battered by risk flight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dagmara Leszkowicz and Jason Hovet   \par \uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2008\par 17:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW/PRAGUE, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Romania's leu recovered a foothold in late trade on Friday after slumping as much as 4 percent earlier in the session due to an emerging market selloff that has battered central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By 1510 GMT, the leu <EURRON=> had lost 1 percent on the day with markets across the region still weighed down by worries over Hungary and a wider global slowdown. The Bucharest stock exchange was temporarily shut after a more than 8 percent drop.\par \par \uc2 At 1417 GMT, Poland's zloty had nudged 0.1 percent down from Thursday's close to 3.84 per euro, well off session lows. The Czech crown <EURCZK=> lost 0.8 percent in illiquid trade.\par \par \uc2 A global sell-off swept through the region's stock markets, with Prague <.PX> down 11.8 percent to its lowest in four years, while Warsaw's blue-chip index <.WIG20> lost 4 percent and touched its lowest level since December 2003.\par \par \uc2 The Bucharest bourse was temporarily shut after a more than 8 percent drop.\par \par \uc2 Unwinding of positions has hit emerging markets this week as the global financial crisis prompted investors to pull money back to major western markets.\par \par \uc2 Worries over Hungary's external financing have sent the forint 15 percent lower since the start of October and forced its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into an emergency 3-percentage-point hike in interest rates on Wednesday. Hungarian markets were closed for a public holiday on Thursday and Friday.\par \par \uc2 The zloty has also shed 14.8 percent in October with heavy falls in the past couple days, leading Polish officials to step in and argue the move is not due to economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Slawomir Skrzypek stood by the bank's long-held hands-off policy on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, saying he did not foresee \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now [ID:nLO201754].\par \par \uc2 "The zloty fell just too much yesterday, and today we're seeing a technical bounce-back connected with the realization of profits," said Jakub Wiraszka, a trader at BRE Bank said.\par \par \uc2 With high local interest rates, Hungarian, Romanian and Polish households have more often opted for loans in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising central Europe's credit exposure.\par \par \uc2 "Given these imbalances and vulnerabilities, the strong tightening in global credit conditions weighs heavily on local markets triggering a mechanical and natural adjustment of exchange rates," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  analyst wrote in a weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report on Friday.\par \par \uc2 STANDING BY\par \par \uc2 Romania's leu <EURRON=> had gained strongly as banks scrambled to meet minimum reserve requirements this week.\par \par \uc2 The Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some cases this month to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and analysts say this has helped lead to a jump in money market rates [ID:nLN111493].\par \par \uc2 In Serbia, the dinar <EURRSD=> fell to a five-month, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  [ID:nLO45368], while \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's kuna <EURHRK=> was steady, but analysts said pressure next week after recent losses could alert the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Polish bond yields dropped on Friday along with Czech yields, with dealers saying some market makers in Prague have started quoting after the Czech bond market seized up over the past month.\par \par \uc2 The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has launched a repo facility that takes government bonds as collateral to boost liquidity, while Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already announced it would offer foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps on a daily basis for banks. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ----------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT-------------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2008 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>   25.078   24.888  -0.76%   +5.36%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>    3.836    3.833  -0.08%   -6.54%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=>  276.570  275.640  -0.34%   -9.38%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>    7.236    7.236   0%      +1.24%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=>    3.634    3.596  -1.06%   -1.5%\par Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>   84.517   83.377  -1.37%   -7.31%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=>\par 3-yr T-bond   CZ3YT=RR   +6 basis points to  173bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   CZ5YT=RR    -9 basis points to  +188bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   CZ9YT=RR   +4 basis points to  +156bps over bmk*\par Polish treasury bonds <0#PLBMK=>\par 2-yr T-bond   PL2YT=RR    -26 basis points to  +467bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   PL5YT=RR    -11 basis points to  +430bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond PL10YT=RR    -36 basis points to 