\par\par \b \uc2 Croatia c.bank buys 56 mln euros to cap kuna  \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2005\par 11:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 ZAGREB, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Croatia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had bought 56 million euros ($67.11 million) from banks at an auction on Friday at an average rate of 7.3799 kuna per euro, hoping to cap the robust local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It was the second \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a lid on the kuna in as many weeks, as the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under appreciation strains after Croatia started EU membership talks on Oct. 4.  \par \par \uc2 The kuna eased to 7.38/7.39 from 7.36/7.37 per one euro after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was floating between 7.40 and 7.45 versus the euro in September, before appreciating in the last two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Increased interest in Croatian securities by foreign investors since the start of EU talks has also considerably contributed to the stronger kuna.  \par \par \uc2 This was the eighth \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year aimed at curbing kuna. In those eight \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a total of 408.9 million euros from commercial banks.  \par \par \uc2 Unlike in the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year did not have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the kuna.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CROATIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|E|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par