\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -High-yielders retreat on dollar revival.  \b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2004\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 8 (Reuters) - A recovery in the dollar caused high-yield emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to retreat from their highs on Wednesday, with falling gold prices and the risk of a rate cut adding to downward pressure on the South African rand. The dollar rallied one percent off Tuesday's record lows against the euro as investors bought back the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the run-up to the year's end and watched the impact of dollar weakness on other economies.  \par \par \uc2 The South African rand was also caught up in a broader commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreat, with the Canadian and Australian dollars falling sharply after both central banks kept interest rates on hold.  \par \par \uc2 "The rand move is very much a reflection of the broad dollar correction and also the sharp gold correction, which has a big impact on the rand," said Tania Kotsos, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Canada.  \par \par \uc2 Bullion prices fell over $7.00 on Wednesday to around $445 per ounce . South Africa is the world's largest gold producer.  \par \par \uc2 By 1046 GMT, the rand was down around 1.5 percent against the dollar at 5.85 .  \par \par \uc2 Concern that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was starting to become more wary of rand strength also weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Tito Mboweni said he preferred a competitive exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts polled by Reuters last week expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep key interest rates steady at 7.5 percent at its monetary policy decision on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 However, the rand's charge to six-year highs against the dollar this month may have raised the chances of a cut, thanks to the dampening effect it has on inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Kotsos said the rand pullback was likely to prove temporary: "This is not trend reversal but more an opportunity to re-enter short dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 "Any correction is likely to see exporters flood in to buy rand." Exporters are natural rand buyers to convert the proceeds of overseas earnings.  \par \par \uc2 Kotsos said key resistance in dollar/rand was at 5.89/5.90 per dollar, with another layer seen at 5.95/5.96.  \par \par \uc2 ZLOTY PEGGED BACK  \par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty also dropped back in line with the broadly soft emerging market tone, with Finance Minister Miroslaw Gronicki casting some doubt on how long the rally could continue.  \par \par \uc2 "We have a floating exchange rate. Today the zloty is strengthening, in 3-4 months the situation may be reversed," he said. Deutsche Bank strategist Caio Natividade said: "Gronicki's statements are interesting in that he seems to be shifting emphasis towards rate cut environment.  \par \par \uc2 "In fact, after recent comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that is already in the process of being priced into the money markets."  \par \par \uc2 He added that didn't change the bank's bullish view on the zloty.  \par \par \uc2 "On a real effective basis versus the euro it may be around fair value, but when you consider other measures such as unit labour costs and the structural appreciation of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region it is still undervalued."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The zloty traded at 4.2040 to the euro  from 4.1778 late on Tuesday and\par at 3.1551 to the dollar        from 3.1116.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This week has seen a string of countries \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Croatia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was the latest on Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Croatian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  called a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tender on Wednesday, its first since mid-September, offering to buy euros from banks to halt the kuna's gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The kuna jumped to 7.46/7.47 per euro , its highest since late October, prior to the tender, following a large euro sell order from a domestic bank on Tuesday. The kuna later eased back to trade at 7.4950/7.5050.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041208e0c80071l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
