\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Turkish lira strong,  \highlight22\uc2 Croatia intervenes \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2004\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, July 6 (Reuters) - The lira traded around two-month highs versus the dollar on Tuesday amid optimism about Turkey's economic outlook, while the Croatian kuna was pushed down against the euro by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Last week's data releases which included first quarter annual economic growth at 12.4 percent and benign inflation gave a broad boost to Turkish markets.  \par \par \uc2 Investor appetite for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also helped the lira since the Federal Reserve's U.S. interest rate hike last week and its pledge for "measured" policy steps in the future.  \par \par \uc2 "The global dynamic is generally more friendly towards high yielders now and this is helping the likes of the Turkish lira," said Callum Henderson, head of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income research at Bank of America in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The domestic picture in Turkey is also supportive."  \par \par \uc2 "We don't expect rampant appreciation but a slow grind lower (in dollar versus lira)," he said.  \par \par \uc2 At 1000 GMT the Turkish lira traded at1.434 million per dollar , up some half a percent on the day and near the 1.43 million area where it set a two-month high earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Headline inflation figures on Saturday showed Turkey's year-end IMF-backed targets were within reach. Consumer prices fell a monthly 0.13 percent, while wholesale prices were down 1.05 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts have said that the price data could encourage Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates within the next month but it is expected to remain cautious in the near term. Official interest rates are at 22 and 27 percent, far above the Fed's 1.25 percent rate.  \par \par \uc2 May industrial production data are due out on Thursday and expected to show annual growth of 16.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 KUNA \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In central and eastern Europe, the Croatian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 18.9 million euros from commercial banks to stem the kuna \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, pushing it further down from recent 22-month peaks against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The kuna eased to 7.36/7.37 on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from a 22-month high of 7.34/7.35. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered to buy euros at a fixed rate of 7.36.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought 208.4 million euros ($256.2 million) from commercial banks so far this year, including another bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, to cap the kuna.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is powered by foreign direct investment flows and the summer tourism season as well as by a new 400 million euro state bond, to be issued on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Israeli shekel pulled back from a late-February high, set at 4.4600 per dollar on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday it traded at 4.4700/4800.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040706e076001h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
