\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Croatia \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to curb kuna rally.   \b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZAGREB, March 17 (Reuters) - The Croatian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open interbank market on Wednesday to try to halt gains in the kuna.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 20 million euros from banks at an auction at an average rate of 7.4975 per euro. The kuna eased marginally immediately after the auction to 7.49/7.50 from 7.47/7.48 where it stood in early trade, though later rose to 7.46/7.47.   \par \par \uc2 The kuna has firmed steadily this month on high demand due to banks' need to maintain their mandatory reserve levels. This year the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced tougher mandatory reserve requirements to further tame the excessive credit expansion largely blamed for the country's high foreign debt.   \par \par \uc2 Banks are obliged to maintain on a daily basis at least a 35 percent coverage of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  liabilities, which puts pressure on their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets.   \par \par \uc2 Appreciation pressures have also increased as the government this month announced the issue of a second tranche of its new 10-year paper RHMF-14, worth 150 million euros for March 23. The first tranche issued in February was worth 200 million euros.   \par \par \uc2 Most Croatian bonds are denominated in euros but paid for in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Demand for the kuna remains strong, so I am not sure how much this new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help liquidity as the banks are not willing to sell larger amount of euros," said a dealer at a major local bank.   \par \par \uc2 "My guess is now that the kuna will float between 7.45 and 7.55 per euro in the coming period," another dealer said.   \par \par \uc2 A month ago the kuna was quoted at 7.67/7.68 against the euro on the open interbank market. The kuna usually peaks in the summer, at the height of the tourist season amid high inflow of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 On the money market, the benchmark overnight lending rate rose to 10.06 percent from 9.94 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Two papers were active on the local bond market in the morning on modest turnover as the strong kuna continued to discourage trade.   \par \par \uc2 Four papers traded on Tuesday on the lowest combined turnover in the last 10 days of mere 29.2 million kuna. Almost half of that, or 14.1 million kuna, was done with the finance ministry's 10-year RHMF-12, yielding 5.18/5.32 percent with the price at a two-week low 110.20/111.20. For details on Tuesday's bond trade see .    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040317e03h001ko\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
