\par\par \b \uc2  Better supply pushes Croatian kuna rate down. \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZAGREB, April 2 (Reuter) - Better supply of kuna funds at lower rates pushed down the Croatian average lending rate on Tuesday to 28.6 percent from 29.0 percent in more active trade. \par \par \uc2  The Zagreb Money Market reported supply rising to 43.6 million kuna from 30.2 million, against 90 million demand. \par \par \uc2  It said 15 million kuna were placements by the state investment bank HBOR at 26 and 27 percent. The interbank lending rate was flat at 30 percent. \par \par \uc2  Most rates declined with only the rate for loans under five days edging up to 30 from 29.4 percent on March 29. \par \par \uc2  Call money, which accounted for most of the money market activities, was down to 29.2 from 29.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  The 31-366 day rate fell to 26 from 30 percent on March 27 and the five-10 day rate eased 20 basis points to 29.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  Overnight settlements to eight banks with shortfalls totalled 213.5 million kuna last night. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate has been fixed at 19 percent, in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today. It calculated the kuna midrate at 5.4701 to the dollar and at 3.6938 to the German mark. -- Kolumbina Bencevic, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-4557075 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4209od5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
