\par\par \b \uc2  Croatian overnight rate falls to 30.5 vs 31.6 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZAGREB, Feb 22 (Reuter) - The Croatian overnight rate fell to 30.5 percent on Thursday from 31.6 percent in line with a decline in the main kuna interbank rates on which it is based. \par \par \uc2  Call money fell to 30.1 from 32.5 percent. The rate for five-10 day loans was down 30 basis points to 30.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the 15-30 day rate rose to 30 from 29.2 percent on February 16. \par \par \uc2  Total settlements through the Zagreb Money Market shrank to 19 million kuna from 30.9 million yesterday. Demand was reported unchanged at 50 million kuna. \par \par \uc2  Last night, with the help of 206.4 million kuna overnight funds all seven commercial banks with shortfalls managed to square their positions without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2  held no \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction today as the kuna held stable against the main hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It calculated the kuna medium rate at 5.3725 to the dollar and at 3.6970 to the German mark. -- Kolumbina Bencevic, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-4557075 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2m019km\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
