\par\par \b \uc2  Croatian overnight rate rises to 29.8 vs 29.5 pct. [CORRECTED 16:22 GMT] \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZAGREB, Feb 1 (Reuter) - The Croatian overnight rate, a weighted average of kuna lending rates during the day, jumped to 29.8 from 29.5 percent in thin interbank trade on the money market. \par \par \uc2  Call money eased to 29.9 from 30 percent, but the rate for five-10 day loans rose 30 basis points to 29.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  (Corrects rate for five-day loans). \par \par \uc2  The Zagreb Money Market reported settlements at 16.8 million kuna, with a surplus of 37 million kuna. \par \par \uc2  Overnight supply also overshot demand by 78 million last night. \par \par \uc2  Loans totalled 247.7 million kuna and all banks managed to square their position without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said banks showed no interest in buying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its auction. \par \par \uc2  It calculated the medium kuna rate at 5.5297 to the dollar and at 3.7077 to the German mark. -- Kolumbina Bencevic, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-4557075 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds2101e89\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
