\par\par \b \uc2  Croatia interbank rate jumps to 25.4 from 24.6 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZAGREB, Dec 14 (Reuter) - The Croatian interbank rate jumped to 25.4 from 24.6 percent, pushed by an overwhelming demand for kuna funds on the money market. \par \par \uc2  The Zagreb Money Market reported a demand for 125 million kuna against only 14.4 million kuna supply, some of which was settled at a rate of 30 percent. \par \par \uc2  Call money rose to 28.4 from 26 percent on Wednesday and five-10 day rate went up to 24.2 percent from 23.9. \par \par \uc2  The rate for 31-366 day loans moved down to 24.3 from 26 percent on December 1. \par \par \uc2  Overnight loans, made at the average daily lending rate, totalled 87.1 million kuna, leaving a shortage of 240.6 million kuna at two commercial banks. \par \par \uc2  This was partly covered by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan to one bank, while the other had to use mandatory reserve to square its position. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2  grants \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to banks three times a month for a maximum of two days. \par \par \uc2  It said it had no \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market today. The bank calculated its weighted exchange rate for the kuna at 5.3691 to the dollar and 3.7172 to the German mark. \par \par \uc2  -- Kolumbina Bencevic, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-4557075 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drce00nrv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
