\par\par \b \uc2  Croatia  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  raises reserve requirement. \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZAGREB, May 9 (Reuter) - Croatia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set a new higher reserve requirement for banks, to regulate money flows, said Adolf Matejka, director for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the National Bank of Croatia. \par \par \uc2  "The reserve ratio has been raised to 29.8 percent from 28.3 PErcent currently," he said \par \par \uc2  In November, the bank introduced a single 28.30 percent reserve ratio for banks and a new 7.50 percent ratio for other savings institutions. The former replaced 27.50 percent applied previously on sight deposits and deposits of up to three months, and 16 percent on deposits exceeding three months. \par \par \uc2  Matejka told Reuters the latest raise in the ratio had been approved to cut down excessive money supply growth and create more space for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the coming period. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Croatia\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets a large inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during summer tourist season, increasing demand for domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at commercial banks and money changers. \par \par \uc2  To compensate for the higher reserve requirement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to pay 5.50 percent interest on obligatory reserve instead of 5.15 it has paid to commercial banks so far. -- Kolumbina Bencevic, Zagreb Newsroom, 385-1-457075 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr5907yfw\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \tro